{"name":"Parrot Tulip","hex":"#eebfd5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"parrot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of bird, many species of which are colorful and able to mimic human speech, of the order Psittaciformes or (narrowly) of the family Psittacidae.","A parroter; a person who repeats the words or ideas of others."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To repeat (exactly what has just been said) without necessarily showing understanding, in the manner of a parrot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/parrot"},{"term":"tulip","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of flowering plant, genus Tulipa.","The flower of this plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tulip"}],"entries":[{"title":"Parrot tulip","blocks":[{"text":"Parrot tulips are a tulip cultivar group known for their bright colors and petals. The petals have a \"serrated\" or \"fringed\" look. The flowers bloom in late spring and reach a size of almost 5 inches (13 cm) in length. Although they are perennials by nature٫ they are often grown as annuals. Parrot tulips come in a wide variety of colors, usually being brightly colored, but can come in darker colors. The flowers originated in France and were brought to the Netherlands in the 18th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Tulipa_%27Apricot_Parrot%27_2016_16.JPG/330px-Tulipa_%27Apricot_Parrot%27_2016_16.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parrot_tulip","wikidata":"Q10616541"},{"title":"Parrot","blocks":[{"text":"Parrots, also known as psittacines from the name of the type genus Psittacus, are birds with a strong curved beak, upright stance, and clawed feet. They are classified in four families that contain roughly 410 species in 101 genera, found mostly in tropical and subtropical regions. The four families are the Psittaculidae, Psittacidae, Cacatuidae (cockatoos), and Strigopidae. One-third of all parrot species are threatened by extinction, with a higher aggregate extinction risk than any other comparable bird group. Parrots have a generally pantropical distribution with several species inhabiting temperate regions as well. The greatest diversity of parrots is in South America and Australasia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Rainbow_lorikeet_%28Trichoglossus_moluccanus_moluccanus%29_Sydney.jpg/330px-Rainbow_lorikeet_%28Trichoglossus_moluccanus_moluccanus%29_Sydney.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parrot","wikidata":"Q31431"},{"title":"Tulip","blocks":[{"text":"Tulips are spring-blooming perennial herbaceous bulbiferous geophytes in the Tulipa genus. Their flowers are usually large, showy, and brightly coloured, generally red, orange, pink, yellow, or white. They often have a different coloured blotch at the base of the tepals, internally. Because of a degree of variability within the populations and a long history of cultivation, classification has been complex and controversial. The tulip is a member of the lily family, Liliaceae, along with 14 other genera, where it is most closely related to Amana, Erythronium, and Gagea in the tribe Lilieae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/%D7%A6%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%9D.JPG/330px-%D7%A6%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%9D.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulip","wikidata":"Q93201"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Tulip_%28Flaming_Parrot_cultivar%29.jpg/500px-Tulip_%28Flaming_Parrot_cultivar%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Tulip_%28Flaming_Parrot_cultivar%29.jpg/1280px-Tulip_%28Flaming_Parrot_cultivar%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Tulip_%28Flaming_Parrot_cultivar%29.jpg/500px-Tulip_%28Flaming_Parrot_cultivar%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An 'Estella Rivnfeld' flower","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATulip_(Flaming_Parrot_cultivar).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parrot_range_2026.png/500px-Parrot_range_2026.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parrot_range_2026.png/1280px-Parrot_range_2026.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Parrot_range_2026.png/500px-Parrot_range_2026.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParrot_range_2026.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/UCMP_143274.png/500px-UCMP_143274.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/UCMP_143274.png/500px-UCMP_143274.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fossil dentary specimen UCMP 143274 restored as a parrot (left) or an oviraptorosaur","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUCMP_143274.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Cyrilavis_colburnorum_FMNH_specimen.jpg/500px-Cyrilavis_colburnorum_FMNH_specimen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Cyrilavis_colburnorum_FMNH_specimen.jpg/1280px-Cyrilavis_colburnorum_FMNH_specimen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser